  <section>
    <p class="wide">
      Core already covers well-formed MIME, reusable configuration and message rules, TLS and OAuth2, diagnostics, conversion, and simple batches.
      Add an optional module only when you need pooled or clustered delivery, authenticated SOCKS, DKIM, S/MIME, OpenPGP/MIME, Outlook conversion,
      command-line use, Spring integration, or Karaf support. Angus is already included as the default SMTP provider when you depend on
      <code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="core-module" class="with-back-to-top">Core</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Core capabilities">
    <a href="features.html#section-basic-usage"><span>Compose</span><strong>Messages and recipients</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="features.html#section-attachments"><span>Content</span><strong>Attachments and images</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="security.html#section-authentication-methods"><span>Transport</span><strong>TLS and OAuth2</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="features.html#section-converting"><span>Interoperate</span><strong>EML and MIME conversion</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>
  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        The main <code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail</code> dependency already includes the core module and the supported Angus sending
        runtime. Most applications need only that one dependency, plus any optional feature modules they actually use.
      </p>
      <p>
        Integration modules such as Spring already depend on the main library, so you do not need to declare it twice. Feature modules can be combined.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1em">The provider-neutral API layer contains:</p>
      <ul class="indent">
        <li><code>org.simplejavamail:core-module</code>: message and mailer API contracts</li>
        <li><code>jakarta.mail:jakarta.mail-api</code>: the standard mail API</li>
        <li><code>com.sanctionco.jmail:jmail</code>: address validation</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The main artifact adds <code class="inline language-text">angus-mail-provider-module</code> at runtime. That adapter brings Angus Mail, while keeping Angus-specific SMTP handling inside the adapter.</p>
      <p>Use the published POM on Maven Central as the authoritative list of transitive dependencies for the current release.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1em">
        As of 9.0.0, the <code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail</code> and <code class="inline language-text">core-module</code> jars include
        Java module descriptors. Modular applications can require
        <code class="inline language-java">org.simplejavamail</code> directly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <div class="doc-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scrollable Simple Java Mail artifact path">
        <table class="artifact-path-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it adds</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th><code>simple-java-mail</code></th><td>The public API and default Angus sending setup</td></tr>
            <tr><th><code>core-module</code></th><td>Provider-neutral API and message model</td></tr>
            <tr><th><code>angus-mail-provider-module</code></th><td>Default SMTP adapter and Angus runtime</td></tr>
            <tr><th>Feature modules</th><td>DKIM, S/MIME, OpenPGP/MIME, pools, proxies, conversion, and integrations as needed</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2 id="angus-mail-provider-module" class="with-back-to-top">Angus Mail provider adapter</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Angus Mail provider adapter capabilities">
    <a href="features.html#section-bouncing-emails"><span>Envelope</span><strong>Bounce address handling</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="features.html#section-delivery-status-notification"><span>Request</span><strong>Delivery status notifications</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="features.html#section-submission-receipts"><span>Inspect</span><strong>Enhanced SMTP responses</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="#mail-transport-adapter-spi"><span>Replace</span><strong>Another Jakarta Mail provider</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
    <div class="view">
      <p>
        You do not normally add this artifact yourself. <code class="inline language-text">simple-java-mail</code> includes it as a runtime dependency, so
        the normal setup remains one dependency and uses Angus as the supported default Jakarta Mail implementation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The adapter is the small provider-specific part of the library. It translates envelope sender, DSN, and detailed SMTP response handling into
        Angus behavior without making the public message, conversion, DKIM, S/MIME, or OpenPGP APIs depend on Angus classes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Only exclude the adapter when you supply another compatible Jakarta Mail implementation. No other provider adapter ships with Simple Java Mail
        today. The Jakarta Mail API jar is not enough by itself: MIME and EML conversion also use implementation services from Jakarta Mail and Jakarta
        Activation. Without an implementation, conversion stops with an error that tells you to restore the default module or add a replacement.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ordinary messages can use Jakarta Mail's regular Transport API with a replacement implementation. Provider-specific envelope or DSN features
        need a matching adapter and fail clearly when the selected provider cannot supply them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="side">
      <p>The adapter brings:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://eclipse-ee4j.github.io/angus-mail/"><code>org.eclipse.angus:angus-mail</code></a></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You do not need a direct Angus dependency for normal sending. Add <code>org.eclipse.angus:angus-mail</code> as a compile dependency only when
        your own source imports <code>org.eclipse.angus.*</code>. A modular application must also require <code>org.eclipse.angus.mail</code>. The
        adapter still supplies Angus at runtime, but deliberately does not make its implementation classes part of your application's compile API.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <a href="#mail-transport-adapter-spi" id="mail-transport-adapter-spi" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Integrating another Jakarta Mail provider</h3>

  <section>
    <p>
      Adding another compatible Jakarta Mail implementation changes the provider behind the Session; it does not require replacing the whole Mailer.
      For ordinary content without provider-specific envelope options, Simple Java Mail can submit through Jakarta Mail's regular
      <code class="inline language-java">Transport.sendMessage(...)</code> API.
    </p>
    <p>
      Implement the public <code class="inline language-java">MailTransportAdapter</code> SPI when the provider must also handle envelope sender or DSN
      options, preserve finalized or protected message bytes, or expose its final SMTP response. The adapter receives an already connected Transport and
      the prepared mail, then returns a provider-neutral <code class="inline language-java">MailTransportResult</code>. Simple Java Mail continues to own
      transport acquisition, connection reuse, pooling, and result translation.
    </p>
    <p>
      An adapter implements <code class="inline language-java">supports(Transport)</code>, optionally declares which content-preservation requirements it
      supports, and performs the final submission in <code class="inline language-java">sendMessage(Transport, PreparedMail)</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <p>Register the implementation through the standard Java service-provider mechanism:</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small"># META-INF/services/org.simplejavamail.api.mailer.spi.MailTransportAdapter
com.example.ExampleMailTransportAdapter</code></pre>
      <p>Or, on the module path:</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">provides org.simplejavamail.api.mailer.spi.MailTransportAdapter
    with com.example.ExampleMailTransportAdapter;</code></pre>
      <p>
        Simple Java Mail selects exactly one adapter whose <code class="inline language-java">supports(...)</code> method matches the concrete Transport.
        Multiple matches fail instead of depending on classpath order.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2 id="batch-module" class="with-back-to-top">Batch and clustering support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Batch module capabilities">
    <a href="features.html#section-sending-asynchronously"><span>Schedule</span><strong>Asynchronous sending</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html"><span>Choose</span><strong>Pool orchestration options</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-clustering"><span>Route</span><strong>Multiple SMTP clusters</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-mailer-lifecycle"><span>Close</span><strong>Mailer lifecycle</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
    <p>
      Add this library for pooled and clustered sending:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;batch-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This adds the following 3rd party dependencies:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/simple-java-mail/smtp-connection-pool">simple-java-mail:smtp-connection-pool</a></li>
    </ul>
	  <p class="wide">
      The module sends work asynchronously and orchestrates the optional <code>smtp-connection-pool</code> dependency. Use the
      <a href="smtp-connection-pooling.html">pooling and batch orchestration guide</a> to compare the full Mailer integration, the standalone
      callback facade, direct leases, and the Jakarta Mail or Camel provider paths. See
      <a href="configuration.html#section-batch-and-clustering">Mailer configuration</a> for its settings.
    </p>
	  <p class="wide">
		  A live connection pool keeps the JVM running. See <a href="configuration.html#section-mailer-lifecycle">Mailer lifecycle and resource ownership</a>
		  for orderly shutdown, including clustered Mailers.
	  </p>
  </section>

