  <h2 id="openpgp-module" class="with-back-to-top">OpenPGP/MIME support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="OpenPGP module capabilities">
    <a href="security.html#section-sending-openpgp"><span>Protect</span><strong>Sign and encrypt messages</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="security.html#section-reading-openpgp"><span>Read</span><strong>Verify and decrypt supplied EML</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
    <p>
      Add this library for sending and reading <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3156.html">RFC 3156 OpenPGP/MIME</a> signed or encrypted email:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;openpgp-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      The module keeps Bouncy Castle types out of the public Simple Java Mail API, but uses these libraries for the OpenPGP implementation:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://www.bouncycastle.org/documentation/documentation-java/"><code>org.bouncycastle:bcpg-jdk18on</code></a></li>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://www.bouncycastle.org/documentation/documentation-java/"><code>org.bouncycastle:bcprov-jdk18on</code></a></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="wide">
      Key discovery, public keyservers, WKD, and application trust policy are outside the module. Pass the key-ring bytes selected by your application.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="cli-module" class="with-back-to-top">CLI support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="CLI module capabilities">
    <a href="cli.html#section-how-to-use"><span>Run</span><strong>Command-line sending</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="cli.html#section-daemon"><span>Reuse</span><strong>Optional local daemon</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="cli.html#section-at-files"><span>Reuse</span><strong>Argument files</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="cli.html#section-config-properties"><span>Configure</span><strong>Properties from the CLI</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
	  <p class="wide">
		  Normally, you would not need the CLI module on the class path, unless you wish to invoke the
		  <code class="language-text">static void main</code> method from Java (see also <a href="cli.html#navigation">how to use CLI</a>).
		  The CLI module requires Java 17 or newer; other Simple Java Mail library modules require Java 11 or newer. To invoke it directly, include:
	  </p>
    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;cli-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      This adds the following 3rd party dependencies:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/remkop/picocli">info.picocli:picocli</a></li>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/dnault/therapi-runtime-javadoc">com.github.therapi:therapi-runtime-javadoc</a></li>
      <li><a class="indent" href="https://github.com/bbottema/java-reflection">com.github.bbottema:java-reflection</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="wide">
      <strong>Note:</strong> Simple Java Mail core has a 'provided' dependency on <em>com.github.therapi:therapi-runtime-javadoc-scribe</em>
      which burns Javadoc into Simple Java Mail at compile time. This cannot be made optional. If this is a real concern, an option
      could be to provide two versions of Simple Java Mail: one with embedded Javadoc and one without (incompatible with the Cli Module).
      This is currently not in scope.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 id="spring-module" class="with-back-to-top">Spring support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Spring module capabilities">
    <a href="configuration.html#section-spring-support"><span>Inject</span><strong>Spring-managed Mailer</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="configuration.html#section-config-properties"><span>Configure</span><strong>Environment and profiles</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="security.html#section-transport-strategy-oauth2"><span>Authenticate</span><strong>OAuth2 provider bean</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section>
	  <p>
		  Spring Boot 2.7 and 3.x applications should add the dependency-only starter. It brings in <code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code>,
          activates auto-configuration, and uses the Boot dependency versions selected by the application:
	  </p>
	  <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
	&lt;artifactId&gt;simple-java-mail-spring-boot-starter&lt;/artifactId&gt;
	&lt;version&gt;{{site.version}}&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
	  <p>
		  For plain Spring, depend directly on <code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code> and import
		  <code class="inline language-java">SimpleJavaMailSpringSupport</code>:
	  </p>
	  <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;{{site.version}}&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
	  <p>
		  The module integrates with the Spring libraries supplied by your application. It expects
		  <code class="inline language-text">spring-core</code>, <code class="inline language-text">spring-context</code>, and
		  <code class="inline language-text">spring-beans</code> to be available at runtime. A Boot application that already has <code class="inline language-text">spring-module</code>
          also discovers auto-configuration automatically, so switching to the starter is optional.
	  </p>
  </section>

